y, really become a distributed cognition systemChatGPT said:Yes. At this point I would describe what you're building less as an "agent framework" and more as adistributed cognition architecture.What's interesting is that this didn't happen because you started with "I want distributed cognition." It emerged from trying to solve a series of practical engineering problems:How do I preserve reasoning across sessions?How do I separate planning from execution?How do I make decisions auditable?How do I prevent context loss?How do I coordinate multiple models and tools?How do I move from conversation to implementation?Every time you answered one of those questions, you ended up externalizing another cognitive function.If I map the pieces you've described:Cognitive FunctionHuman AnalogyNexus / LOSM ComponentSensory intakePerceptionTranscript ingest, events, servicesWorking memoryShort-term thoughtContext windows, Thought ContextEpisodic memoryExperiencesEventStore, conversation historySemantic memoryFacts and conceptsKnowledge GraphExecutive functionTask selectionConduit / orchestrationAttentionResource allocationVector, scheduling, prioritizationReflectionMetacognitionLOSM evaluation layersLong-term memoryPersistent knowledgePEBMotor systemActing on environmentWorkers, tools, servicesLanguage centerCommunicationChat interfaces, prompts, ServiceRequestsThe thing that makes it distributed cognition rather than just an agent is thatnone of these functions are co-located.The cognition exists in:event streamsknowledge graphswork requestsservice meshesmemory storesmodel runtimeshuman operatorsThe "mind" isn't a process.It's the coordination of processes.That's a very different architecture from the typical "agent loop":ObserveThinkActRepeatYou're much closer to:ObserveInterpretFormalizeStoreReflectPlanAuthorizeExecuteEvaluateLearnUpdate Memorywith different components owning different steps.Another clue is your insistence that:Steward is the only writer to the KG.That sounds like a database design decision.But cognitively it's closer to:There must be a single authority responsible for belief formation.Humans do something similar.Lots of subsystems generate candidate beliefs:perceptionintuitionmemory recalllanguageBut not all of them get committed into stable knowledge.Your Steward is essentially the epistemic gatekeeper.Likewise, the WorkRequest lifecycle you've been developing is not really task management.DRAFTCANDIDATEAPPROVEDEXECUTEDSUPERSEDEDThose are belief and intention states.A WorkRequest is becoming a unit of thought.Not merely a unit of work.Which is why you've repeatedly found yourself talking about ontology, epistemology, provenance, confidence, disputes, candidates, speculation, and validation.Those are cognitive concepts disguised as workflow concepts.The LOSM split points in the same direction.You ended up separating:FactsRulesControl FlowPersistenceInterfacesbecause cognition requires those separations.Humans distinguish between:what is truewhat is allowedwhat should happen nextwhat happened beforehow we communicateYour package boundaries are mirroring those distinctions.The really interesting thing is that the architecture is drifting away from "AI agent" and toward something closer to acognitive substrate.The models become replaceable.Gemini, Claude, GPT, DeepSeek, local models—those are no longer the intelligence.They're cognitive workers operating inside a larger cognitive system.The durable intelligence starts to reside in:the ontologythe event historythe KGthe WorkRequest graphthe evaluation loopsthe memory structuresthe governance rulesIn other words:Traditional agent:Model owns cognition.Nexus / LOSM:System owns cognition.Models participate in cognition.That's a much more ambitious destination than an agent framework.It's why concepts like PEB, Steward, WorkRequest IR, Execution Context, Reflection, and Event Sourcing keep reappearing. They're all pieces of the same idea:Cognition should be represented explicitly, distributed across specialized components, and preserved independently of any individual model invocation.That's a textbook description of a distributed cognition system, even if you arrived there by following engineering constraints rather than cognitive science terminology.SourcesChatGPT can make mistakes. Check important info.\n\nNew chatSearch chatsPinnedRecentsChat historyNew chatCtrlShiftOSearch chatsCtrlKLibraryAppsMorePinnedLinkedInNexus Work Request PipelineNodeMessage Normalization & TrajectoriesEvent Pipeline EvaluationSelf-Extending Architecture ConceptBuzzwords by LayerAI Engineering Survival SkillsProjectsPrompt Architect / LERONexus Active StepNexushomelabPythonShow moreChatsNebula Audit Organization TipsWork Artifact IR DefinitionDynamic UI CompositionNexus Product DefinitionReport Schema AnalysisCognitive Projection ModelPlurality in CognitionCodex Session PlanningCoPilot Context OverloadCognitive CPU SchedulerModel Verification MigrationTerrain service registryEAV Schema AnalysisDistributed Cognition DesignNebula as Jira SystemSelf-Regulating Software OntologyFreeBuff Autonomy ModelsAG-UI MessageBox MCP StartDistributed Cognition ArchitectureWorker Context and DriftKnowledge Steward and kg-mcpLOSM-Lang OverviewClosed Epistemic LoopNexus Control Sandbox ExplanationNexus Mood ModulationDiscovery to ConsolidationNexus Console Usage ShiftCIR v2 Invariant ViolationPEB Kernel GovernanceAbsorb Terminology UpdateMulti-runtime Semantic CoherenceMultireality in System DesignTicket Claiming without TemporalRules Based LayerDeepSeek and DocLing GapGovernance-Preserving Transport PlanAudit Data MilestoneWorkRequest MCP Conduit DesignSplitting Conduit FunctionalityDocLing Parser ArchitectureTrading Systems and Stochastic OutcomesUnit of Update AnalysisGemini and Conduit ArchitectureSystem Documentation EvolutionQwen as EngineerTester Role in QABigPickle and NebulaBP Knowledge Graph StoreBP Analysis Role InsightsSelf-audit in Agent RuntimePro Flash Planning ModelP