false}], "codeArtifacts": [], "sequenceNumber": 2, "architecturalIntent": "Define Nexus's architecture as an artificial organization: Nexus = Event Brain (event substrate + projections), OpenCode = Hands (filesystem manipulation), Ollama/Qwen = Reasoning Cortex (LLM-based generation), Filesystem/Compiler = Reality (the ground truth of what works), Tests = Physics Laws (the invariant verifier). This makes Nexus an 'artificial organization' rather than a tool — it coordinates cognitive labor the way an organization coordinates human labor, with defined roles, authority, and accountability."}, {"title": "Three-Layer Agent Economy — Compute (Energy) → Attention (Allocation) → Trust (Credit)", "status": "Agreed", "followUps": [], "requirements": [{"text": "Three layers: Resource (energy), Attention (allocation), Trust (credit)", "checked": false}, {"text": "Tokens = fuel, not money", "checked": false}, {"text": "Schedulers = central banks of attention economy", "checked": false}, {"text": "Trust reduces approval overhead — credit for agents", "checked": false}, {"text": "All three layers must exist for stable agent society", "checked": false}], "codeArtifacts": [], "sequenceNumber": 3, "architecturalIntent": "Define the three-layer economy that emerges in mature agent systems: (1) Resource Economy — tokens, GPU time, API calls, disk IO, execution slots (equivalent to fuel/energy). (2) Attention Economy — decision attention as the scarce resource: which task runs first, which proposal gets reasoning time, which agent gets context allocation. Schedulers quietly become central banks. (3) Trust Economy — agents earn trust operationally through reliability and accuracy. Trust reduces oversight cost. Trusted agents consume fewer approvals. All three layers must exist for stable institutional growth."}, {"title": "Institutional Economy — Legitimacy as the Ultimate Currency in Agent Systems", "status": "Agreed", "followUps": [], "requirements": [{"text": "Legitimacy = acceptance layer → gates authority", "checked": false}, {"text": "Tokens = enables action, Compute = enables execution, Trust = enables autonomy", "checked": false}, {"text": "Legitimacy = enables authority", "checked": false}, {"text": "Event kernel, civil service, token economy enforce legitimacy", "checked": false}, {"text": "Legitimacy infrastructure: mechanisms, approvals, flows that make authority operational", "checked": false}], "codeArtifacts": [], "sequenceNumber": 4, "architecturalIntent": "Recognize that once compute, tokens, trust, and attention economies exist, legitimacy becomes the ultimate currency. No matter how clever agents are, if their proposals, actions, and optimizations aren't recognized by the governance structure, they are effectively invisible. Legitimacy determines whether a proposed workflow, ontology change, or agent decision actually matters. It is the acceptance layer that gates authority. The event kernel, civil service, and token economy all become tools to enforce and calibrate legitimacy."}]	# Harvested Specification & Code Repository\n**Source:** /home/codex/dev/chats/Nexus - LLM-as-Operating-Agent.html\n**Model:** DeepSeek V4\n**Total candidates:** 4\n---\n## 1. Embodied Agency — Intelligence Exists Because It Can Act and Observe Consequences\n**Status:** `Agreed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nDefine embodied agency as the architectural principle that intelligence exists because it can act and observe consequences — not because it can talk. Nexus agents gain embodiment through OpenCode integration: they read the actual repo, edit real files, run compilers and tests, observe results, and retry. The model describes changes (representational) is replaced with the model acts inside reality (operational). This is the foundation for organizational intelligence: the system learns from actual outcomes, not prompt feedback.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Agency through action and consequence observation\n- [ ] OpenCode as embodiment layer — reads/writes real filesystem\n- [ ] Organizational intelligence from actual outcomes, not prompt feedback\n- [ ] Act > describe — operational AI replaces representational AI\n- [ ] System learns from environment feedback, not human evaluation\n\n---\n\n## 2. Nexus as Artificial Organization — Event Brain + Hands + Reasoning Cortex + Reality + Physics Laws\n**Status:** `Agreed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nDefine Nexus's architecture as an artificial organization: Nexus = Event Brain (event substrate + projections), OpenCode = Hands (filesystem manipulation), Ollama/Qwen = Reasoning Cortex (LLM-based generation), Filesystem/Compiler = Reality (the ground truth of what works), Tests = Physics Laws (the invariant verifier). This makes Nexus an 'artificial organization' rather than a tool — it coordinates cognitive labor the way an organization coordinates human labor, with defined roles, authority, and accountability.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Event Brain: event substrate coordinates all cognition\n- [ ] Hands: OpenCode manifests actions as filesystem changes\n- [ ] Reasoning Cortex: LLMs generate plans and solutions\n- [ ] Reality: filesystem and compiler as ground truth\n- [ ] Physics Laws: tests as invariant verifiers\n- [ ] Organization model replaces tool model\n\n---\n\n## 3. Three-Layer Agent Economy — Compute (Energy) → Attention (Allocation) → Trust (Credit)\n**Status:** `Agreed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nDefine the three-layer economy that emerges in mature agent systems: (1) Resource Economy — tokens, GPU time, API calls, disk IO, execution slots (equivalent to fuel/energy). (2) Attention Economy — decision attention as the scarce resource: which task runs first, which proposal gets reasoning time, which agent gets context allocation. Schedulers quietly become central banks. (3) Trust Economy — agents earn trust operationally through reliability and accuracy. Trust reduces oversight cost. Trusted agents consume fewer approvals. All three layers must exist for stable institutional growth.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Three layers: Resource (energy), Attention (allocation), Trust (credit)\n- [ ] Tokens = fuel, not money\n- [ ] Schedulers = central banks of attention economy\n- [ ] Trust reduces approval overhead — credit for agents\n- [ ] All three layers must exist for stable agent society\n\n---\n\n## 4. Institutional Economy — Legitimacy as the Ultimate Currency in Agent Systems\n**Status:** `Agreed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nRecognize that once compute, tokens, trust, and attention economies exist, legitimacy becomes the ultimate currency. No matter how clever agents are, if their proposals, actions, and optimizations aren't recognized by the governance structure, they are effectively invisible. Legitimacy determines whether a proposed workflow, ontology change, or agent decision actually matters. It is the acceptance layer that gates authority. The event kernel, civil service, and token economy all become tools to enforce and calibrate legitimacy.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Legitimacy = acceptance layer → gates authority\n- [ ] Tokens = enables action, Compute = enables execution, Trust = enables autonomy\n- [ ] Legitimacy = enables authority\n- [ ] Event kernel, civil service, token economy enforce legitimacy\n- [ ] Legitimacy infrastructure: mechanisms, approvals, flows that make authority operational\n\n---\n	{harvest,deepseek-v4}	{}	2026-06-21 02:09:39.319529+00	2026-06-21 02:09:39.319529+00	2026-06-21 02:09:39.319529+00	9999-12-31 23:59:59+00
