ce: local storage + API save/load\n\n---\n	{nexus_component_creator_tab_spec,harvest,rover-batch,deepseek}	{"slug": "nexus_component_creator_tab_spec", "model": "DeepSeek V4 (manual extraction)", "source_chat": "Nexus - Component Creator Tab Spec", "has_source_text": true}	2026-06-21 05:59:50.401792+00	2026-06-21 05:59:50.401792+00	2026-06-21 05:59:50.401792+00	9999-12-31 23:59:59+00
7661f8f5-d1fa-4e1a-a5df-7ff0bd0a8e29	`chats/Nexus - Database Deprecation Reasons.html`	Nexus - Database Deprecation Reasons.html`	DeepSeek V4 (manual extraction)	3	[{"title": "Micro-Repository Pattern Critique — Verbose, Rigid, Non-Scalable", "status": "Observed", "requirements": ["Micro-repositories: FindAll/FindOne/Save/Delete per entity", "Database interface: service locator/facade for all entity repos", "Scales poorly: 4N getters for N entities", "Special queries break the strict CRUD pattern", "Parallel helpers required for non-standard operations"], "code_snippets": [], "open_questions": [], "intent_description": "Analyze a deprecated Database interface pattern that exposed FindAll/FindOne/Save/Delete interfaces for each entity type via explicit getters. The pattern looked elegant initially (uniform CRUD access) but scaled poorly: each new entity type added four getters, special queries broke the pattern, and parallel helper classes were required. This is the abstraction scaling trap — initial elegance becomes maintenance burden as the entity universe grows.", "implementation_notes": []}, {"title": "Repository<T, ID> as Modern Alternative — Generic, Type-Safe, Extensible", "status": "Agreed", "requirements": ["Generic Repository<T, ID> interface", "findById, findAll, save, delete core operations", "Custom queries via per-entity extension", "Dependency injection for selective repository access", "Compatible with Spring Data, Micronaut Data"], "code_snippets": [], "open_questions": [], "intent_description": "Replace the micro-repository facade with a generic Repository<T, ID> pattern. Single interface per entity with findById/findAll/save/delete operations. Custom queries added per-entity via extension. Generic repositories are injected via DI (Spring/Micronaut) where needed, eliminating the monolithic Database facade. This scales cleanly: 1 interface per entity, not 4N methods.", "implementation_notes": []}, {"title": "Abstraction Scaling Trap — Rigid Micro-DSL Patterns vs Flexible Generics", "status": "Observed", "requirements": ["Micro-DSLs feel elegant at 1-5 variants", "At 50+ variants, micro-DSLs collapse under boilerplate", "Generic abstractions accommodate variance without breaking", "Design for scaling from day 1 — generic > specific", "Communicate invariants, not mechanisms"], "code_snippets": [], "open_questions": [], "intent_description": "Abstract the lesson from the Database facade and the Flyweight-for-5-trade-types stories: ultra-specific micro-DSL patterns feel elegant initially but become maintenance nightmares when reality scales unexpectedly (5 trade types → 102). Generic but flexible abstractions (Repository<T, ID>, discriminated unions) scale better because they accommodate variance without breaking the base pattern.", "implementation_notes": []}]	# Harvested Specification & Code Repository\n\n**Source:** `chats/Nexus - Database Deprecation Reasons.html`\n**Model:** DeepSeek V4 (manual extraction)\n**Batch:** 7\n**Total candidates:** 3\n\n---\n\n## 1. Micro-Repository Pattern Critique — Verbose, Rigid, Non-Scalable\n**Status:** `Observed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nAnalyze a deprecated Database interface pattern that exposed FindAll/FindOne/Save/Delete interfaces for each entity type via explicit getters. The pattern looked elegant initially (uniform CRUD access) but scaled poorly: each new entity type added four getters, special queries broke the pattern, and parallel helper classes were required. This is the abstraction scaling trap — initial elegance becomes maintenance burden as the entity universe grows.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Micro-repositories: FindAll/FindOne/Save/Delete per entity\n- [ ] Database interface: service locator/facade for all entity repos\n- [ ] Scales poorly: 4N getters for N entities\n- [ ] Special queries break the strict CRUD pattern\n- [ ] Parallel helpers required for non-standard operations\n\n---\n\n## 2. Repository<T, ID> as Modern Alternative — Generic, Type-Safe, Extensible\n**Status:** `Agreed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nReplace the micro-repository facade with a generic Repository<T, ID> pattern. Single interface per entity with findById/findAll/save/delete operations. Custom queries added per-entity via extension. Generic repositories are injected via DI (Spring/Micronaut) where needed, eliminating the monolithic Database facade. This scales cleanly: 1 interface per entity, not 4N methods.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Generic Repository<T, ID> interface\n- [ ] findById, findAll, save, delete core operations\n- [ ] Custom queries via per-entity extension\n- [ ] Dependency injection for selective repository access\n- [ ] Compatible with Spring Data, Micronaut Data\n\n---\n\n## 3. Abstraction Scaling Trap — Rigid Micro-DSL Patterns vs Flexible Generics\n**Status:** `Observed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nAbstract the lesson from the Database facade and the Flyweight-for-5-trade-types stories: ultra-specific micro-DSL patterns feel elegant initially but become maintenance nightmares when reality scales unexpectedly (5 trade types → 102). Generic but flexible abstractions (Repository<T, ID>, discriminated unions) scale better because they accommodate variance without breaking the base pattern.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Micro-DSLs feel elegant at 1-5 variants\n- [ ] At 50+ variants, micro-DSLs collapse under boilerplate\n- [ ] Generic abstractions accommodate variance without breaking\n- [ ] Design for scaling from day 1 — generic > specific\n- [ ] Communicate invariants, not mechanisms\n\n---\n	{nexus_database_deprecation_reasons,harvest,rover-batch,deepseek}	{"slug": "nexus_database_deprecation_reasons", "model": "DeepSeek V4 (manual extraction)", "source_chat": "Nexus - Database Deprecation Reasons", "has_source_text": true}	2026-06-21 05:59:50.513784+00	2026-06-21 05:59:50.513784+00	2026-06-21 05:59:50.513784+00	9999-12-31 23:59:59+00
6d434cb5-01df-4398-a536-dad18f715281	`chats/Nexus - EA Tools vs Coding.html`	Nexus - EA Tools vs Coding.html`	DeepSeek V4 (manual extraction)	2	[{"title": "EA Tools as PowerPoint with Data Flow — Nexus as Code-Native Architecture", "status": "Agreed", "requirements": ["EA tools = document-first, code-second architecture modeling", "Nexus = code-native, live, executable architecture", "Diagrams are optional outputs, not primary artifacts", "Nexus: small footprint, high leverage ratio", "TypeSpec: machine-verifiable contracts vs manual EA governance", "EA tools as 'Google Earth for architecture' — reference, not implementation"], "code_snippets": [], "open_questions": [], "intent_description": "Traditional EA tools (Archi, ArchiMate, Ardoq, Orbus, Enterprise Architect) are essentially structured slide decks with connectors — document-first, not code-first. They map and visualize enterprise architecture for multi-stakeholder visibility, compliance, and governance. Nexus takes the inverse approach: architecture lives in code, diagrams are optional outputs, and the system is executable rather than documented. Nexus is a 'small sharp tool' with high leverage ratio — easy to use, cheap to run. TypeSpec adds the microscopic precision EA tools missed: formalized, machine-verifiable contracts.", "implementation_notes": []}, {"title": "Power vs Leverage — Traditional EA vs Code-Native Architecture", "status": "Agreed", "requirements": ["EA: high effort, moderate leverage — bureaucratic overhead", "Nexus: low effort, high leverage — executable architecture", "Code-native > document-first for system evolution", "TypeSpec: formalize, validate, generate — replace manual governance", "Live architecture > static diagrams"], "code_snippets": [], "open_questions": [], "intent_description": "Traditional EA tools occupy the high-effort, moderate-leverage quadrant — full suite of governance, reporting, and diagramming overhead designed for bureaucratic leverage, not technical leverage. Nexus/Alloy.js/TypeSpec sits in the low-effort, high-leverage zone: small, sharp, precise tools that formalize, validate, and generate artifacts automatically. The missing plot EA tools missed by millimeters is that architecture should be live, codified, and type-checked, not just drawn.", "implementation_notes": []}]	# Harvested Specification & Code Repository\n\n**Source:** `chats/Nexus - EA Tools vs Coding.html`\n**Model:** DeepSeek V4 (manual extraction)\n**Batch:** 8\n**Total candidates:** 2\n\n---\n\n## 1. EA Tools as PowerPoint with Data Flow — Nexus as Code-Native Architecture\n**Status:** `Agreed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nTraditional EA tools (Archi, ArchiMate, Ardoq, Orbus, Enterprise Architect) are essentially structured slide decks with connectors — document-first, not code-first. They map and visualize enterprise architecture for multi-stakeholder visibility, compliance, and governance. Nexus takes the inverse approach: architecture lives in code, diagrams are optional outputs, and the system is executable rather than documented. Nexus is a 'small sharp tool' with high leverage ratio — easy to use, cheap to run. TypeSpec adds the microscopic precision EA tools missed: formalized, machine-verifiable contracts.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] EA tools = document-first, code-second architecture modeling\n- [ ] Nexus = code-native, live, executable architecture\n- [ ] Diagrams are optional outputs, not primary artifacts\n- [ ] Nexus: small footprint, high leverage ratio\n- [ ] TypeSpec: machine-verifiable contracts vs manual EA governance\n- [ ] EA tools as 'Google Earth for architecture' — reference, not implementation\n\n---\n\n## 2. Power vs Leverage — Traditional EA vs Code-Native Architecture\n**Status:** `Agreed`\n\n### Architectural Intent\nTraditional EA tools occupy the high-effort, moderate-leverage quadrant — full suite of governance, reporting, and diagramming overhead designed for bureaucratic leverage, not technical leverage. Nexus/Alloy.js/TypeSpec sits in the low-effort, high-leverage zone: small, sharp, precise tools that formalize, validate, and generate artifacts automatically. The missing plot EA tools missed by millimeters is that architecture should be live, codified, and type-checked, not just drawn.\n\n### Requirements & Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] EA: high effort, moderate leverage — bureaucratic overhead\n- [ ] Nexus: low effort, high leverage — executable architecture\n- [ ] Code-native > document-first for system evolution\n- [ ] TypeSpec: formalize, validate, generate — replace manual governance\n- [ ] Live architecture > static diagrams\n\n---\n	{nexus_ea_tools_vs_coding,harvest,rover-batch,deepseek}	{"slug": "nexus_ea_tools_vs_coding", "model": "DeepSeek V4 (manual extraction)", "source_chat": "Nexus - EA Tools vs Coding", "has_source_text": true}	2026-06-21 05:59:50.643013+00	2026-06-21 05:59:50.643013+00	2026-06-21 05:59:50.643013+00	9999-12-31 23:59:59+00
